[direction] d-04 -> blocked (The tool budget ran out. The FLAMINGO -0.1/-0.2, X-COP f_nth 6% and 1-b = 0.85, and Planck B ~0.6/0.8 values come from abstracts and have no verbatim cite() quotes in references.bib (dead end x-03). No X-COP profile values were quoted, plot_observable_comparison was not run, and the feedback spread from d-06 is still pending.)
**Blocked — Synthesis: apply the non-thermal pressure correction to the emulated HSE masses, and compare the corrected and uncorrected b_HSE and the scaled low-z profiles with FLAMINGO, X-COP and SPT benchmarks..** (The tool budget ran out. The FLAMINGO -0.1/-0.2, X-COP f_nth 6% and 1-b = 0.85, and Planck B ~0.6/0.8 values come from abstracts and have no verbatim cite() quotes in references.bib (dead end x-03). No X-COP profile values were quoted, plot_observable_comparison was not run, and the feedback spread from d-06 is still pending.) I computed corrected and uncorrected b_HSE at both redshifts, but not every benchmark number has a cite() quote, the X-COP profile comparison was not done and no comparison plot was made. Inputs are the four fiducial CSVs in /Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/ramp-research-cluster-profiles/runs/261020_110147/ (CGD and CTP at z=0 and z=0.4987) and references.bib. All arithmetic below was done by hand from the CSV values, not run in a tool.

1. Uncorrected (thermal-only) mass at R500 (f-012). Slopes are finite differences between the 0.866 and 1.155 R500 bins. At z=0: density slope -2.545, temperature slope -0.583, T/T500 = 0.592, so M_HSE/M500 = 0.925 and b_HSE = -0.075. At z=0.4987: density slope -2.738, temperature slope -0.505, T/T500 = 0.518, so M_HSE/M500 = 0.840 and b_HSE = -0.160. b_HSE follows the FLAMINGO convention, (M_HSE - M500)/M500. The mass shift from z=0 to z=0.4987 due to profile shape alone is -9.2%. The absolute values assume kT500 = G mu m_p M500/(2 R500), which the tool does not confirm.

2. Non-thermal-corrected mass (f-013). I used the Nelson+14 f_nth formula (A=0.452, B=0.841, gamma=1.628) with P_tot = P_th/(1 - f_nth), so M_tot/M500 = (t/2)/(1-f) * [-(s_n + s_T) + dln(1-f)/dln r]. To convert radii I assumed an NFW profile with c200c=4 and Omega_m=0.3, giving R200m/R500c = 2.60 at z=0 and 1.94 at z=0.4987. f_nth at R500 is 0.207 at z=0 and 0.260 at z=0.4987. M_tot/M500 is 1.093 at z=0 and 1.035 at z=0.4987, so corrected b_HSE is +0.09 and +0.035. This overcorrects: the mass ends up above M500. f_nth of about 21-26% at R500 is well above the X-COP median of about 6% at R500. The correction raises the mass by a factor of 1.18 at z=0 and 1.23 at z=0.4987, and shrinks the redshift shift to -5.3%. That reduction comes entirely from the assumed change in R200m/R500c with redshift.

3. Which effect dominates the redshift trend (f-014). Profile shape dominates: the scaled temperature at R500 falls 12.4%, partly offset by a 3.7% steeper slope sum. The non-thermal correction changes the size of the trend only through the radius-conversion assumption. Feedback cannot be ranked yet because d-06 is still pending. The lower HSE mass at higher redshift agrees in sign with FLAMINGO's cluster trend (quote in references.bib).

4. SPT comparison. The emulated temperature falls by 12% at R500 and 30% at 2.7 R500 between z=0 and 0.5. SPT finds clusters at 0.6<z<1.2 about 40% cooler than at 0.3<z<0.6 (quote in references.bib). The redshift ranges do not overlap, so this is only qualitative. No X-COP T/T500 or scaled-density values were ever quoted, so I made no X-COP profile comparison.

5. Limitations: no test of sensitivity to the analytic fitting form; no direct-gradient versus fitted-profile comparison; the profiles are a stacked mass bin of unknown mass; the temperature weighting and T500 definitions are unknown; and the c200c=4 conversion between R200m and R500c is an assumption.
[usage]
[direction] d-05 -> closed
**Completed — Put the headline fiducial numbers on tool provenance and settle the f-004, f-005 and f-008 conflicts. Load the four fiducial CSVs (CGD and CTP at z=0 and z=0.4987), show every bin from 0.3 to 2.74 R500, and recompute the R500 slopes, t(R500), M_HSE,500c/M500 and the shape-only shift. Use one stated error convention and the FLAMINGO sign convention..** All four fiducial CSVs were read in full with read_text_file (tc-...-94 to -97), so every bin from 0.015 to 2.74 R500, with mean and y_std, now appears in tool output. load_table_data cannot be used on these files because it fails on the CSV header (dead end x-04). Files: /Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/ramp-research-cluster-profiles/runs/261020_110147/C{GD,TP}_z{0.0000,0.4987}_prediction.csv. Slopes are finite differences in ln y versus ln r on a grid with spacing 0.2878. The derived numbers were computed by hand from those values; no tool calculated them.

Results at R500, from the 0.866 and 1.155 bins (geometric midpoint 1.000):
- z=0: dln rho/dln r = -2.545, dln T/dln r = -0.583, t(R500) = 0.592, M_HSE,500c/M500 = 0.925, b_HSE = -0.075.
- z=0.4987: dln rho/dln r = -2.738, dln T/dln r = -0.505, t(R500) = 0.518, M_HSE,500c/M500 = 0.840, b_HSE = -0.160.
- b_HSE uses the FLAMINGO sign convention, (M_HSE - M500)/M500. The absolute values assume kT500 = G mu m_p M500/(2 R500), which the tool does not confirm.

At the 0.487 bin, central difference over 0.365 and 0.649:
- z=0: -1.863 and -0.271, M/M500 = 0.408.
- z=0.4987: -1.872 and -0.345, M/M500 = 0.382.
- The -1.98 in f-008 is the one-sided pair 0.487-0.649, so it is the slope at 0.562 R500, not 0.5 R500.

At the 1.54 bin:
- z=0: -2.929 and -0.912, M/M500 = 1.272.
- z=0.4987: -3.097 and -1.072, M/M500 = 1.221.

Errors are linear propagation of the GP y_std, bracketed two ways:
- Adjacent bins independent (upper bound): M_HSE/M500 = 0.925 +- 0.049 at z=0 and 0.840 +- 0.047 at z=0.4987.
- Adjacent bins fully correlated (lower bound): 0.925 +- 0.010 and 0.840 +- 0.018.
- The +-0.3 in T500 units (+-0.15 M500) in f-008 overstates the error; the independent-bin value is +-0.10 in T500 units, i.e. +-0.05 M500.

Shape-only shift in M_HSE,500c from z=0 to z=0.4987: -9.2%. Significance is 1.3 sigma (+-7.0%) with independent bins and 4.3 sigma (+-2.1%) with fully correlated bins. The real GP covariance between bins is unknown, so the true significance lies between these bounds.

Findings f-015, f-016 and f-017 replace f-004, f-005 and f-008.

Still open: the GP covariance between bins (a Monte Carlo would need a Python tool), the emulator's T500 definition, and the slope at R200 under a proper R200/R500 conversion (1.54 R500 is still assumed to be R200).
